\section{Identify the Interface as Well as the Slot}

A \texttt{Thermometer} can implement both
\texttt{Readable} and \texttt{Calibrated}. If slot zero
of the first interface is \texttt{read} and slot zero
of the second is \texttt{calibrate}, the number zero
alone does not identify the requested operation. The
interface identity determines the namespace for the slot.
The receiver determines which implementation of that
interface to use. Implementations can represent that
second mapping as a lookup from a class, or arrange for
it to be carried with the receiver.

The JVM's \texttt{invokeinterface} refers to a resolved
interface method and selects a target relative to the
receiver class. Its rules do not require one physical
layout. Rust's book explains the contrasting source
choice between use of a generic bound and a trait object.
The following example shows one possible representation of
the latter idea rather than claiming a stable Rust ABI.

\section{Package the Data Pointer With Evidence of Conformance}

A borrowed erased value in our language is a pair
\((p,w)\): \(p\) denotes a concrete receiver, and \(w\)
denotes the witness table for that receiver's conformance
to a particular interface. The table contains call
entries compiled for an erased receiver ABI. Each entry
knows how to pass its receiver to the concrete body.
The introduction rule, which constructs the pair, must
verify the correspondence between data and witness.
\Cref{fig:visual-406} shows how the two halves of the
pair are used.

