A borrowed pair does not by itself own the data. An
owned package needs destruction and deallocation rules,
and may include size, alignment, or tracing metadata.
Some designs separate ownership support from dispatch
slots. Decide whether a witness has process lifetime or
can become stale when a defining library unloads. A
valid pointer to receiver bytes is not proof the
associated code remains callable.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Check Conformance at Erasure and Adapt at the Call}

\begin{verbatim}
erase_borrow(receiver : borrow T, contract):
    checked := resolve_conformance(T, contract)
    table := materialize_witness(checked, erased_abi(contract))
    return BorrowedPair(address(receiver), table, lifetime(receiver))

call_read(value : Readable<Output = Int>, meter):
    slot := slot_of(Readable<Output = Int>, "read")
    entry := value.witness.methods[slot]
    return entry(value.data, meter)

thermometer_read_adapter(data, meter):
    receiver := as_borrowed_thermometer(data)
    return thermometer_read(receiver, meter)

upcast(pair, super_contract):
    conversion := pair.witness.super_views[super_contract]
    return BorrowedPair(conversion.adjust(pair.data),
                        conversion.witness, pair.lifetime)
\end{verbatim}

In the example, the associated output type is fixed by
the erased contract: every allowed witness returns an
integer using the same calling convention. If one witness
returned a byte array instead, the caller could not
choose how to receive or drop it. Other solutions include
returning a boxed value with its own metadata or making
the associated type unavailable in callable erased
requirements. A method quantified over a fresh type at
each call cannot be implemented by an ordinary fixed
slot for every concrete instantiation unless the ABI
defines an additional universal calling protocol.

\section{Do Not Reuse a Witness Across an Unproved Conversion}

Upcasting to a superinterface does not generally mean
copying the pair unchanged. If the target's slots are
not a prefix of the original view, the witness pointer
must change. Some implementations reuse an appropriate
prefix; others store a superinterface witness explicitly.
Adapt the data pointer too if the target witness expects
another view. The witness and pointer form an invariant
together. Do not update one before writing it to a
shared container if a reader could observe the mismatch.

\begin{verbatim}
thermometer as Readable<Int>:
    witness R0: slot 0 = read_thermometer
thermometer as Calibrated:
    witness C0: slot 0 = calibrate_thermometer
call Calibrated slot 0 with R0:
    not a representable well-typed operation
\end{verbatim}

Construct a heterogeneous sequence containing a simulated
sensor and a thermometer. Compare the output from erased
reads with direct calls and verify their adapters receive
the intended concrete addresses. Add a type with the
same-sized representation and deliberately different
read behavior. A deduplicator cannot collapse the two
witnesses merely because the allocations are equal in
size. A default interface implementation may itself
need the witness to call another requirement; passing
evidence onward ensures that a concrete override is used.

In a dictionary-passing generic, the same conformance
information can arrive in a hidden argument while the
value retains a non-erased static type. A witness table
and a trait object are not synonyms: one is evidence,
while the other packages evidence with data and a
lifetime or ownership contract. Test an owned form by
destroying it after the last call and observing the
concrete destructor exactly once. Test the borrowed form
by transferring the pair between functions but rejecting
its use after the borrow ends.

\section{Pass the Witness Into Shared Default Code}

Now extend \texttt{Readable} with a default \texttt{readTwice}.
It calls \texttt{read} twice and combines the results.
Suppose the thermometer produces a reading from stored
state, while a simulated sensor advances a deterministic
sequence on each read. The default body can be generated
once using erased operands and a self-witness, or it can be
specialized for each conformance. It cannot call
\texttt{thermometer\_read} in shared code merely because
that was the first conformance loaded. The fixed output
type lets the same body combine the results regardless
of where the readings came from.

\begin{verbatim}
shared_read_twice(data, self_witness, meter):
    read := self_witness.methods[READ_SLOT]
    first := read(data, meter)
    second := read(data, meter)
    return (first, second)

thermometer_default(data, meter):
    return shared_read_twice(data, Thermometer_Readable, meter)

simulator_default(data, meter):
    return shared_read_twice(data, Simulator_Readable, meter)
\end{verbatim}

Those two default adapters have the same entry ABI as an
override. If only the simulator supplies an explicit override
of \texttt{readTwice}, replace its default adapter with its
own callable entry, without touching its \texttt{read}
slot. The operation ID lives in the interface contract; the
choice of default or concrete code lives in the conformance.
Calling shared default code can itself perform another
dispatch, so an optimizer that folds the outer call must
separately establish the target of each inner operation.

As a test, install a \texttt{read} that logs its invocation and
returns increasing integers. Invoking the shared default
must log twice, in order, and return two different values.
Erasing the sensor as a superinterface should still reach
its concrete read adapter through the new witness view.
If an erased value crosses a library boundary, verify that
both parties agree on the witness version and slot
signature; matching a prefix of method names says nothing
about associated types or ownership conventions.

A good negative fixture asks to erase two readers whose
associated outputs differ into the same \texttt{Readable<Int>}
sequence. Reject the incompatible conversion when the
witness would be formed, not at the eventual indirect
call. The caller's return convention must already be known.
